course: dictionary
lesson: 2498
title: Debate: discuss and argue
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Debate is a word with more than one job. As a noun, a debate is a formal discussion where people argue for an idea and others argue against it, often with a vote at the end. Look for clues like a speaker, a side, or a vote.
explain: Debate can also mean ordinary disagreement. «Writing was invented separately in Mesopotamia... whether Egypt's writing was separate is debated.» Here debated means people still disagree and talk about it. Nobody has settled the answer yet.
explain: As a verb, to debate means to think hard about which choice to make. «I debated whether to go» means I weighed up the choice before deciding. The word whether is a clue: it shows a question you are thinking about.
fact: A debate is a formal discussion where people give arguments for and against an idea.
fact: «Debate» can also mean discussion and disagreement about something.
fact: To debate is to discuss something carefully, or to think hard about which choice to make.
fact: Phrases to know: «hold a debate», «a heated debate» (people feel strongly and argue hard), «open to debate» (not yet settled).
fact: Parliament and councils debate new laws before they are passed.
exercise: «Our class will hold a debate on whether pets should live in homes.» What is a debate? | A quiet walk around the playground | *A formal discussion where people argue for and against an idea | A test with marks | A game of chess
exercise: «Whether Egypt's writing was separate is still debated.» What does debated mean here? | Painted in bright colours | Finished and agreed by everyone | Written down in a book | *Discussed and disagreed about, with no answer yet
exercise: Priya says, «I debated whether to go.» What does debated mean? | Forgot all about it | *Thought hard about which choice to make | Danced around the room | Shouted loudly at someone
test: «The debate about the new park was heated.» What does heated suggest? | Very hot to touch | Everyone was bored | *People felt strongly and argued with passion | The park was on fire
test: What does «open to debate» mean? | Locked up and hidden | *Not yet settled, so people can still argue about it | Fully agreed by everyone | Free for anyone to enter
exam: «A bill goes through readings (debates), a committee that checks it line by line, and amendments (changes) before it becomes law.» What are the readings (debates) for? | The bill is printed in a newspaper | The bill is sent to the post office | Children read the bill aloud for fun | *Lawmakers discuss and argue about the bill
